    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Traditionally, relative permeability has been measured at conditions of room temperature. Relative permeability curves obtained at room temperature were then used to predict performance at reservoir temperatures. Results of recent relative permeability measurements at elevated temperature show departure from the room temperature curves. Temperature has clearly been shown to affect relative permeability but the results are not conclusive. In some cases, temperature dependence may not occur.Equipment was constructed to perform dynamic displacement experiments on small core samples under conditions of elevated temperature. Oil-water flowing fraction and pressure drop were continuously recorded to permit calculation of both the relative permeability ratio and the individual relative permeabilities. Imbibition relative permeabilities were measured for five samples of Boise sandstone at room temperature and at 175F. The fluids used were distilled water and a white mineral oil.Results for all samples were similar. The irreducible water saturation increased significantly while the residual oil saturation decreased significantly with temperature level increase. The individual relative permeability to oil increased while the individual relative permeability to water decreased with temperature increase.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
